Keep a removed wl_output global alive while clients can still bind it

wl_global_remove only announces that a global is going away; a client that
has not read the announcement yet can still send a bind for it, and binding
a destroyed global is a fatal protocol error ("global wl_output (N) is
unavailable"). Hyprland destroyed a retired output global at once whenever
nothing was bound to it yet. Every client binds a new wl_output the moment
it is announced, so an output that was added, removed and added again within
a roundtrip -- a dock's connector flapping while its tunnel comes up --
disconnected every client at once: Xwayland, the shell, portals, the input
method and every app.

Keep removed globals for five seconds before destroying them, as wlroots
does, and drain resources bound to a global whose monitor is already gone.

Not yet upstream.

